71530::body::003",0.927757],["PMC7871530::body::019",0.913213],["PMC7871530::body::013",0.903465],["PMC10180745::discussion::015",0.879354],["PMC10180745::discussion::017",0.840652],["PMC10180745::conclusion::018",0.538194],["PMC11703406::body::003",0.536799],["PMC10180745::results::009",0.092524],["PMC11703406::body::038",0.060197],["PMC10180745::results::011",0.044101],["PMC11703406::body::006",0.003854]]},{"attempt":0,"llm_sufficient":true,"ms":3547.99,"node":"grade_context","score_ok":true,"sufficient":true,"top_rerank_score":0.984094},{"model_status":"answered","ms":11122.36,"n_claims":5,"node":"generate","valid":true},{"ms":4486.55,"n_claims":5,"node":"verify_citations","removed":1,"valid":true},{"blocked":null,"ms":0.64,"node":"output_guard"}],"status":"answered"},"output":"Some evidence suggests that the increase in lean mass from creatine supplementation is attributed to water retention, and that lean mass may be an imprecise proxy for skeletal muscle mass because it includes body water. [1][2]\nOne study found that creatine supplementation increased TBW and ICW volume compared to placebo, but the ratio of skeletal muscle mass to ICW remained similar in both groups. [3]\nThe increase in lean mass following creatine supplementation has at least partly been attributed to water retention in muscle tissue, with greater osmotic pressure causing muscle cell swelling, which is considered a key stimulus for cell growth. [4]\nIn contrast, ICW is described as an important cellular signal for protein synthesis that drives an increase in muscle mass over time. [3]","timestamp":"2026-10-20T13:34:12.063+00:00","trace_id":"31eb4663-2043-48d5-b3f8-618347c864cf"}
